  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nThe mid level short wave will continue to push east across the upper\nOhio Valley through early afternoon. Fairly widespread light \nprecipitation associated with this will continue through much of the\nmorning before beginning to taper off this afternoon as the short \nwave moves off to the east. Highs today will be in the low to mid \n60s.\n\nAn upper level trough axis will move out of the Plains on Tuesday \nand into the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley region through the day on \nWednesday. Increasing southerly flow ahead of this will lead to \ndecent moisture advection up into our region beginning late Tuesday \nnight and continuing into Wednesday, with fairly widespread shower \nactivity overspreading the Ohio Valley region. With any instability \nlimited at best, expect the thunder threat to be on the low side with\nthe precipitation on Wednesday. Precipitation will then taper off \nfrom the west heading into Wednesday night as the trough axis shifts \neastward.\n\nKey message 2) \nA series of mid level short waves will rotate down across the Great \nLakes/Ohio Valley region as we head into the weekend. \nTiming/placement and strength of these vary though between the \nmodels so there is some uncertainty in pcpn chances and timing \nthrough the second half of the long term period and will generally \njust have some lower end pops going. Some cooler air will filter down\ninto the area though, so we will see a return to more seasonable \ntemperatures for the weekend.",
